---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import aiStack from '../../assets/ai-stack.webp';
import scene1 from '../../assets/ai-stack-scene1.webp';

// The AI Stack Feature — Scene 1 only.
//
// PROTOTYPE. Not registered in src/features/registry.ts, so it has no
// permanent route, no Explore card, no share image, no structured data and
// no sitemap entry. The homepage still renders whatever the registry names
// (currently Zero Trust). The only place this appears is the dev-only
// preview route, /preview/ai-stack/.
//
// The premise: people keep meeting LLM, prompt, context, memory, RAG, tools
// and agents as separate vocabulary. One ordinary errand — "go get what we
// need for dinner" — is the mental model that holds them together. So this
// opening shows the errand and says nothing about the parts. The picture and
// the sentence raise the question; the Explore story, when it exists, is what
// answers it. Deliberately absent here: every term above, any diagram, any
// label on the artwork, any second explanatory paragraph.
//
// Built on the same three-beat composition the Zero Trust Feature uses in
// this slot, in the same editorial grammar: serif for the sentence worth
// carrying away, the site's sans for the thought that helps you understand
// it, cobalt sans plus the saffron circular arrow for the thing you can go
// and explore. No new idiom is introduced.
//
// The artwork is the supplied AIStack master, unaltered, stored as lossless
// WebP: the alpha channel is exact and the colour is bit-for-bit identical
// across all 649,939 visible pixels, at 45% of the PNG's weight in the
// repository. Same treatment src/assets/social/ai-answered-you-share.webp
// already uses.

// ENTERING THE STORY. "See why" no longer goes anywhere. It used to be a
// link to a separate page, and the hard document switch broke the one thing
// this Feature is for: the picture is the question, so throwing it away and
// redrawing the visitor somewhere else made the answer look like a different
// subject. Now the two pictures share one box and one position, and the
// first dissolves into the second — the errand full of floating objects
// resolving into the ordinary moment underneath it. Nothing scrolls, nothing
// jumps, the page does not reload.
//
// It is the site's own two-state technique, unchanged: the same one the
// "looks real, is it?" story uses to hold two images in a single stage and
// cross-fade them by opacity. Same duration, same easing.
//
// THE NEXT BEAT — still inside Scene 1, still no navigation. "Got it" is
// the natural, unremarkable response to being told to get dinner things: of
// course you got it. Only once that's said does the gap become visible —
// you understood an instruction that told you almost nothing. So this beat
// doesn't leave the picture, it turns the same scene over: the request
// fades, the artwork that illustrated it quiets so it stops asking to be
// read, and what's left is the realisation and the question it raises.
// "What's missing? →" is where this stops — it has no handler, on
// purpose, until the beat after it is built.
//
// THE UNSPOKEN DECISIONS — the beat "What's missing? →" opens, still
// without leaving this scene or this page, and without growing it: this
// beat transforms the picture's own box rather than adding to the column
// beneath it, so nothing here ever requires scrolling. The kicker and
// bridge line — having done their job — fade the way "Got it" and the
// request before them did. What replaces them is not more copy in the
// tail: it is five short questions, placed directly over the artwork's
// own quiet margins, around the two people already standing there — the
// decisions the errand handed over without ever being said out loud,
// surfacing where they actually happened. They arrive one at a time and
// then stay, and once all five have landed, the realisation appears at
// the centre of the same picture, larger and plainer than anything
// around it.
//
// Three flags, one per beat (data-entered, then data-realized, then
// data-unsaid), each gating the click that sets the next — the same
// one-way, set-once pattern the Explore story itself uses. The turn
// buttons ("Got it", "What's missing?") are the story's own page-turn
// idiom — serif text, saffron circle, the same one "So you go" and "What
// didn't she say?" use on the story page — not the cobalt "explore"
// grammar "See why" uses, because nothing here leaves the scene.
---

<div class="dy-hero__ais" data-ais>
  <!-- Two elements, in this order: the picture, then the line that turns
       it into an invitation. There is no title above the artwork any more.
       That is the whole point of this arrangement — the hero's own
       "Understand the world that's arriving." is the page's only headline,
       and a second sentence set above a second column made the visitor
       choose which one to read first. Now the right column opens with a
       picture, which cannot be read as a competing headline, and the only
       words in it arrive underneath as a caption-scale invitation.
    -->

  <!-- The artwork, used exactly as supplied: nothing drawn over it, no
       label baked in, no background behind it, no text. It carries the
       whole SEE IT on its own — an ordinary grocery run.

       It is now the feature's hook rather than its illustration, so it
       takes the whole column instead of the 600px measure it shared with a
       headline, and it is the first thing in the column.

       Measured off the asset's alpha channel rather than its box: the
       painted illustration starts 6.05% down the canvas and runs to the
       very bottom edge, and spans 5.3%-95.7% across. Almost no empty sky
       and no empty floor, so the stage is simply the PNG's own 3:2 frame,
       and only that thin 4% of sky (as a share of width) is taken back out
       of the gap below it. -->
  <div class="dy-hero__ais-stage">
    <Image
      class="dy-hero__ais-art dy-hero__ais-art--stack"
      src={aiStack}
      alt="A shopper pushing a full grocery cart, a handwritten list in one hand, with the things the errand needed floating around them."
      widths={[600, 760, 920, 1120, 1400]}
      sizes="(min-width: 1320px) 700px, 92vw"
      format="webp"
      quality={90}
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
      decoding="async"
      data-art="stack"
    />

    <!-- The scene underneath, in the same box at the same size. Fetched
         with the page rather than on the click, so the cross-fade has
         something to fade to the moment it is asked for — but at low
         priority, so it never competes with the picture actually on
         screen. It is hidden from assistive technology until it is the
         one being shown; the script hands that over with the fade. -->
    <Image
      class="dy-hero__ais-art dy-hero__ais-art--scene"
      src={scene1}
      alt="A young man in a navy hoodie stands listening as his mother, one hand open mid-sentence, speaks to him on her way out."
      widths={[600, 760, 920, 1120, 1400]}
      sizes="(min-width: 1320px) 700px, 92vw"
      format="webp"
      quality={90}
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="low"
      decoding="async"
      aria-hidden="true"
      data-art="scene"
    />

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE UNSPOKEN DECISIONS — laid directly over the artwork's own
         box, not appended below it, so this beat transforms the scene
         in place instead of growing the page. Hidden until earned (see
         the CSS below); once open, nothing here recedes.

         Five short lines, positioned in the artwork's own quiet
         margins — the empty column of canvas to either side of the two
         people, measured off the same alpha channel that placed the
         quote earlier. Plain text, nothing drawn around them: no card,
         no bubble, no pointer connecting a question to a body part,
         which is what would turn this into a diagram. They are meant to
         read as thoughts that were always there, only now visible.

         The realisation is NOT in here — the picture's own canvas runs
         flush to its bottom edge with no clear ground to set it on, and
         forcing it to the centre put it directly over the two people's
         faces. It closes the beat instead in the tail below (see
         .dy-hero__ais-realize), the one place in this composition that
         is genuinely clear, on the same reveal and the same timing. aria-
         live so a screen reader hears each question as it arrives rather
         than only on request. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-unsaid" tabindex="-1" data-unsaid-panel aria-live="polite">
      <!-- Each question carries its own connector: a single thin curved
           stroke, not an arrow — no head, no fill, nothing pointing at a
           specific part of the picture, just a gentle line suggesting the
           thought belongs to the scene it sits beside. Presentation only,
           aria-hidden throughout. -->
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-ask" data-ask="1">
        Which shop?
        <svg class="dy-hero__ais-ask-line" viewBox="0 0 48 30" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M3 4 C 16 4, 30 14, 45 26" />
        </svg>
      </p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-ask" data-ask="2">
        Where is everything?
        <svg class="dy-hero__ais-ask-line" viewBox="0 0 48 30" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M45 4 C 32 4, 18 14, 3 26" />
        </svg>
      </p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-ask" data-ask="3">
        Sold out?
        <svg class="dy-hero__ais-ask-line" viewBox="0 0 40 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M2 4 C 16 2, 26 10, 38 16" />
        </svg>
      </p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-ask" data-ask="4">
        Are the tomatoes good?
        <svg class="dy-hero__ais-ask-line" viewBox="0 0 48 30" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M3 26 C 16 26, 30 16, 45 4" />
        </svg>
      </p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-ask" data-ask="5">
        How do you pay?
        <svg class="dy-hero__ais-ask-line" viewBox="0 0 40 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M38 4 C 24 2, 14 10, 2 16" />
        </svg>
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE SWAP — "See where this goes" replaces the whole scene in
         place rather than adding beneath it: the picture, the five
         questions and the realisation below all fade out completely
         (see their [data-bridge='1'] rules), and this one short line
         takes over the exact same box the picture occupied, centred
         both ways rather than sitting under where the artwork was.
         Nothing named yet — no LLM, no prompt, no agent — this is only
         the turn, not the explanation. tabindex="-1" so focus has
         somewhere to land when the button that opened this disappears. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-swap" tabindex="-1" data-bridge-panel>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-swap-text">
        <span class="dy-hero__ais-swap-setup">That grocery run?</span>
        <span class="dy-hero__ais-swap-payoff">AI works more like that than you might think.</span>
      </p>

      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature. Fades out completely once used (see its own
           [data-showme='1'] rule), the same way "See where this goes"
           faded once it opened this beat. -->
      <button type="button" class="dy-hero__ais-turn dy-hero__ais-swap-next" data-showme>
        Show me <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE PROMPT — "Show me" replaces this scene the same way "See
         where this goes" replaced the one before it: the previous copy
         and its own control fade out completely (see their
         [data-showme='1'] rules), and this takes over the same box,
         centred the same way. The instruction the visitor already read
         back in Scene 1 returns first, so what follows reads as "that
         thing, named" rather than a new idea. The name itself arrives
         in one plain sentence, not a heading or a badge — "prompt" gets
         only a subtle weight lift within the same line, using the
         existing type system's own heavier register rather than a new
         idiom (no uppercase, no colour, no box). Nothing else named yet
         — no LLM, no context, no memory, no RAG, no tools, no agent, no
         orchestration — and no next control yet either. tabindex="-1"
         so focus has somewhere to land when "Show me" disappears. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-prompt" tabindex="-1" data-prompt-panel aria-live="polite">
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-prompt-quote">&ldquo;Go get the things we need for dinner.&rdquo;</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-prompt-line">
        In AI, that instruction is called a <span class="dy-hero__ais-prompt-word">prompt</span>.
      </p>

      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature. Fades out completely once used (see its own
           [data-context='1'] rule), the same way "Show me" faded once
           it opened this beat. -->
      <button type="button" class="dy-hero__ais-turn dy-hero__ais-prompt-next" data-context>
        But that wasn&rsquo;t enough <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE CONTEXT — "But that wasn't enough" replaces this scene the
         same way each turn before it has: the prompt beat and its own
         control fade out completely (see their [data-context='1']
         rules), and this takes over the same box. Two ordinary facts
         arrive first — the kind of thing a visitor already knows to
         factor into "the things we need" without being told to — then
         the line that names the practical difference they make, then
         the one sentence that names the category itself, the same
         understated way "prompt" arrived: a subtle weight lift within
         an otherwise plain sentence, not a heading or a badge. No image,
         no card, no definition. Nothing else named yet — no memory, no
         RAG, no tools, no agent, no orchestration — and no next control
         yet either. tabindex="-1" so focus has somewhere to land when
         "But that wasn't enough" disappears. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-context" tabindex="-1" data-context-panel aria-live="polite">
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-context-fact">
        Dinner is for four.<br />
        Mum is making pasta.
      </p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-context-line dy-hero__ais-context-line--1">Now you know what to buy.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-context-line dy-hero__ais-context-line--2">
        In AI, that extra information is called <span class="dy-hero__ais-context-word">context</span>.
      </p>

      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature. Fades out completely once used (see its own
           [data-memory='1'] rule), the same way every control before it
           faded once it opened its own beat. -->
      <button type="button" class="dy-hero__ais-turn dy-hero__ais-context-next" data-memory>
        And you already knew something else <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE MEMORY — "And you already knew something else" replaces
         this scene the same way each turn before it has: the context
         beat and its own control fade out completely (see their
         [data-memory='1'] rules), and this takes over the same box.
         Another ordinary fact arrives first, then the line that names
         what was true without being said — the same realisation Scene
         1's own kicker used — before the one sentence that names the
         category, the same understated way "prompt" and "context" both
         arrived: a subtle weight lift within an otherwise plain
         sentence, not a heading or a badge.
         No image, no card, no definition. Nothing else named yet — no
         RAG, no tools, no agent, no orchestration — and no next control
         yet either. tabindex="-1" so focus has somewhere to land when
         "And you already knew something else" disappears. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-memory" tabindex="-1" data-memory-panel aria-live="polite">
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-memory-fact">Dad always gets the same biscuits.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-memory-line dy-hero__ais-memory-line--1">Nobody had to tell you this time.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-memory-line dy-hero__ais-memory-line--3">
        AI can remember things from before too. That&rsquo;s <span class="dy-hero__ais-memory-word">memory</span>.
      </p>

      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature, waiting for the beat it leads to. Deliberately not
           wired to anything yet: no data-attribute, no listener; the
           script below never queries it. -->
      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature. Fades out completely once used (see its own
           [data-lookup='1'] rule), the same way every control before it
           faded once it opened its own beat. -->
      <button type="button" class="dy-hero__ais-turn dy-hero__ais-memory-next" data-lookup>
        What if you&rsquo;re not sure? <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE LOOKUP — "What if you're not sure?" replaces this scene the
         same way each turn before it has: the memory beat and its own
         control fade out completely (see their [data-lookup='1'] rules),
         and this takes over the same box. Three short lines walk the
         same ordinary errand through a moment memory alone doesn't
         cover — not knowing, then checking somewhere for the answer,
         then having it — without naming what that is yet. No emphasised
         word this time: nothing is being named, so nothing gets the
         saffron treatment. No image, no card. Nothing named yet — no
         RAG, no retrieval, no tools, no agent, no orchestration — and
         no next control yet either. tabindex="-1" so focus has
         somewhere to land when "What if you're not sure?" disappears. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-lookup" tabindex="-1" data-lookup-panel aria-live="polite">
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-lookup-fact">You can&rsquo;t find the pasta sauce.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-lookup-line dy-hero__ais-lookup-line--1">So you check the aisle signs.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-lookup-line dy-hero__ais-lookup-line--2">Now you know where to look.</p>

      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature, waiting for the beat it leads to. Deliberately not
           wired to anything yet: no data-attribute, no listener; the
           script below never queries it. -->
      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature. Fades out completely once used (see its own
           [data-retrieval='1'] rule), the same way every control before
           it faded once it opened its own beat. -->
      <button type="button" class="dy-hero__ais-turn dy-hero__ais-lookup-next" data-retrieval>
        AI can do something similar <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE RETRIEVAL — "AI can do something similar" replaces this
         scene the same way each turn before it has: the lookup beat and
         its own control fade out completely (see their
         [data-retrieval='1'] rules), and this takes over the same box.
         Two short lines name what the previous beat's errand actually
         showed — not needing to hold everything, being able to go find
         what's missing instead — before the one sentence that names the
         category, the same understated saffron lift "prompt", "context"
         and "memory" all got: only the word, not the sentence. No
         mechanism, no image, no card. Nothing else named yet — no RAG,
         no databases, no search, no embeddings, no documents, no tools,
         no agent, no orchestration — and no next control yet either.
         tabindex="-1" so focus has somewhere to land when "AI can do
         something similar" disappears. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-retrieval" tabindex="-1" data-retrieval-panel aria-live="polite">
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-retrieval-fact">It doesn&rsquo;t have to know everything.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-retrieval-line dy-hero__ais-retrieval-line--1">It can look for the information it needs.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-retrieval-line dy-hero__ais-retrieval-line--2">
        In AI, that&rsquo;s called <span class="dy-hero__ais-retrieval-word">retrieval</span>.
      </p>

      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature, waiting for the beat it leads to. Deliberately not
           wired to anything yet: no data-attribute, no listener; the
           script below never queries it. -->
      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature. Fades out completely once used (see its own
           [data-rag='1'] rule), the same way every control before it
           faded once it opened its own beat. -->
      <button type="button" class="dy-hero__ais-turn dy-hero__ais-retrieval-next" data-rag>
        But finding it is only part of it <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE RAG — "But finding it is only part of it" replaces this
         scene the same way each turn before it has: the retrieval beat
         and its own control fade out completely (see their
         [data-rag='1'] rules), and this takes over the same box. The
         same aisle-sign errand returns as two short facts — finding the
         information, then acting on it — because that pairing is
         exactly what the category names, then the one line that says AI
         does both together, before the closing line that names it: the
         same understated saffron lift every term before it got, only
         the word itself. RAG stays as written — it is an acronym, not a
         word being given emphasis-by-transform — and is not expanded or
         explained here; that is a later beat, not this one. No
         mechanism, no image, no card. Nothing else named yet — no
         databases, no embeddings, no vector search, no documents, no
         tools, no agent, no orchestration — and no next control yet
         either. tabindex="-1" so focus has somewhere to land when "But
         finding it is only part of it" disappears. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-rag" tabindex="-1" data-rag-panel aria-live="polite">
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-rag-fact">
        You checked the sign.<br />
        Then you used what it told you.
      </p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-rag-line dy-hero__ais-rag-line--1">AI can do that too: find what it needs, then use it.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-rag-line dy-hero__ais-rag-line--2">
        That&rsquo;s <span class="dy-hero__ais-rag-word">RAG</span>.
      </p>

      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature, waiting for the beat it leads to. Deliberately not
           wired to anything yet: no data-attribute, no listener; the
           script below never queries it. -->
      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature. Fades out completely once used (see its own
           [data-tools='1'] rule), the same way every control before it
           faded once it opened its own beat. -->
      <button type="button" class="dy-hero__ais-turn dy-hero__ais-rag-next" data-tools>
        But dinner still isn&rsquo;t home <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE TOOLS — "But dinner still isn't home" replaces this scene
         the same way each turn before it has: the RAG beat and its own
         control fade out completely (see their [data-tools='1'] rules),
         and this takes over the same box. Knowing something and finding
         something both stop short of an ordinary evening's actual
         demands — getting somewhere, paying for something — before the
         one line that names what AI does about that gap: the same
         understated saffron lift every term before it got, only the
         word itself. No mechanism, no image, no card, no definition.
         Nothing else named yet — no agent, no orchestration — and no
         next control yet either. tabindex="-1" so focus has somewhere
         to land when "But dinner still isn't home" disappears. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-tools" tabindex="-1" data-tools-panel aria-live="polite">
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-tools-fact">You still have to get dinner home.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-tools-line dy-hero__ais-tools-line--1">
        Maps helps you find the shop.<br />
        Your card helps you pay.
      </p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-tools-line dy-hero__ais-tools-line--2">AI can use things to get a job done too.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-tools-line dy-hero__ais-tools-line--3">
        That&rsquo;s <span class="dy-hero__ais-tools-word">tools</span>.
      </p>

      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature, waiting for the beat it leads to. Deliberately not
           wired to anything yet: no data-attribute, no listener; the
           script below never queries it. -->
      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature. Fades out completely once used (see its own
           [data-plan='1'] rule), the same way every control before it
           faded once it opened its own beat. -->
      <button type="button" class="dy-hero__ais-turn dy-hero__ais-tools-next" data-plan>
        But who decides what to do next? <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE PLAN — "But who decides what to do next?" replaces this
         scene the same way each turn before it has: the tools beat and
         its own control fade out completely (see their [data-plan='1']
         rules), and this takes over the same box. The answer arrives
         first, plainly, then the four ordinary steps the whole errand
         was always made of, revealed one at a time, then the closing
         thought that the visitor was never just following a fixed
         script — a change partway through gets absorbed without the
         errand falling apart. Deliberately no AI language anywhere in
         this beat: no term is being named, so nothing here gets the
         saffron treatment, and none of these lines emphasise a single
         word the way every beat since "prompt" has. No image, no card.
         tabindex="-1" so focus has somewhere to land when "But who
         decides what to do next?" disappears. -->
    <div class="dy-hero__ais-plan" tabindex="-1" data-plan-panel aria-live="polite">
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-plan-fact">You do.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-plan-step dy-hero__ais-plan-step--1">You decide where to go.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-plan-step dy-hero__ais-plan-step--2">You get what you need.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-plan-step dy-hero__ais-plan-step--3">You pay.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-plan-step dy-hero__ais-plan-step--4">You head home.</p>
      <p class="dy-hero__ais-plan-line">And if something changes, you adjust.</p>

      <!-- The next turn — same idiom as every other page-turn in this
           Feature. Fades out completely once used (see its own
           [data-agentic='1'] rule), the same way every control before it
           faded once it opened its own beat. -->
      <button type="button" class="dy-hero__ais-turn dy-hero__ais-plan-next" data-agentic>
        What if AI could do that part too? <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================
         AGENTIC AI — "What if AI could do that part too?" replaces this
         scene the same way each turn before it has: the plan beat and
         its own control fade out completely (see their [data-agentic='1']
         rules), and this takes over the same box. The goal arrives
         first, then the three-part answer the plan beat just walked
         through in human terms, then the name for it, with the same
